// A store whose receiver IS a string LITERAL has no binding that could
// own a copy of the literal's bytes: the bytes live in the program's
// static string pool, and every evaluation of the site names the same
// bytes. The store would therefore write into the program's constant
// data -- a shared mutable constant.
//
// A binding that takes a literal over DOES own a copy (SPEC §9.2), so
// `string a = "abc"; a[0] = 65;` is legal and lands in the binding's own
// copy. Only the literal itself is refused.
void main() {
    "abc"[0] = 65;
    printline("unreachable");
}
// expect-diagnostic: Cannot store into a string literal
